course: dictionary
lesson: 2363
title: A word with a hard punch
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A word can be a thing, an action, or part of a phrase. Context tells you which. In the story, «the point of the Rat's cudgel struck something that sounded hollow.» Here cudgel is a thing, a stick, because the Rat can hold it and it can strike something.
explain: Look for clue words around the word. In «the great ugly cudgel in his grasp», the word grasp means a tight hold, so a cudgel is something you hold in your hand. Ugly and grasp are the clues, and they tell you it is a weapon, not a tool for building.
explain: Watch for phrases. If you read «Cudgel your brains», a stick cannot think. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Think very hard to remember something" fits, so the phrase is about thinking, not about hitting. Always test the meaning in the sentence to be sure it makes sense.
fact: A cudgel is a short, thick stick used as a weapon; it is another word for a club.
fact: As a verb, «cudgel» means to hit someone with a cudgel.
fact: «Cudgel your brains» means think very hard to remember something.
fact: «Take up the cudgels for someone» means defend them strongly in an argument.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Badger, Ratty, Mole and Toad arm themselves with cudgels and pistols to win back Toad Hall from the weasels and stoats.
exercise: In the story, Ratty swings «the great ugly cudgel in his grasp». What is a cudgel here? | A kind of front door | A soft cushion for a chair | A small flower in a garden | *A short thick stick used as a weapon
exercise: Mum says, «I had to cudgel my brains to remember the code.» What does she mean? | She shouted the code out loudly | She forgot the code completely | *She thought very hard to remember it | She hit her head gently
exercise: Mia took up the cudgels for her little brother when a bully teased him. What does this phrase mean? | *She defended him strongly | She hit the bully with a stick | She ran away and hid from the bully | She forgot about him
test: In the story, the guard tried to cudgel the robber. What does cudgel mean as a verb here? | To think very hard | *To hit someone with a stick | To give someone a present | To hide quietly behind a tree
test: «Here and there wicked little faces popped out of holes, but vanished immediately at sight of the valorous animal.» What does valorous mean? | Silly | Tired | Hungry | *Brave
exam: Which sentence uses cudgel to mean a weapon? | *The robber carried a heavy cudgel through the dark wood. | The children had to cudgel their brains for the answer. | She gave a cudgel her best smile. | The team wanted to cudgel the other side in the game.
